*CloseUI: *ARSaveToner
*% *****************************************************************************

*% **** Color Mode *************************************************************
*% Renamed from Sharp's own "ARCMode" to CUPS's standard "ColorModel"
*% keyword -- same PostScript setcolormode calls, same choices, just
*% re-keyed. This is the actual fix (not new behavior): CUPS's IPP layer
*% only auto-translates the standard print-color-mode job attribute --
*% what a client's Color/Grayscale dialog selection actually becomes on
*% the wire -- into a PPD option specifically named "ColorModel". With
*% the vendor's original "ARCMode" name, that translation silently never
*% happened at all, so a job's own color choice was always ignored in
*% favor of whatever this option's queue-wide default happened to be --
*% confirmed live: a job explicitly sent as black-and-white still showed
*% up in the copier's own Job Log as color, because CMAuto's per-page
*% auto-detection (the previous default) is what actually decided, not
*% the sender's selection.
*OpenUI *ColorModel/Color Mode: PickOne
*OrderDependency: 180 AnySetup *ColorModel
*DefaultColorModel: Gray
*ColorModel Auto/Automatic: "
	userdict /ARCMode known not {userdict /ARCMode 0 put} if
	0 setcolormode"
*End
*ColorModel RGB/Color: "
	userdict /ARCMode known not {userdict /ARCMode 1 put} if
	1 setcolormode"
*End
*ColorModel Gray/Black and White: "
	userdict /ARCMode known not {userdict /ARCMode 2 put} if
	userdict /ARCIntent known not {userdict /ARCIntent 4 put} if
	userdict /ARCNGray known not {userdict /ARCNGray 2 put} if
	userdict /ARCSConv known not {userdict /ARCSConv 0 put} if
	userdict /ARCPureB known not {userdict /ARCPureB 2 put} if
	2 setcolormode"
*End
*CloseUI: *ColorModel
*% *****************************************************************************

